'''"LOST CORNER" FOOTAGE: THE SCENE KTK DIDN'T WANT YOU TO SEE''' — Director Kondo Akira's 12 minutes of cut footage has been viewed 8.4 million times since July 10. The footage shows a subplot about government pressure on a small business owner — a subplot Kondo says he was told to remove for being "too political." KTK denies instructing Kondo. Kondo contradicts KTK. Communications Committee reviewing. #TellTheTruth at 5.1M. The Okami Times notes: the footage is public. The truth is coming. The question is whether the system that told Kondo to remove it will be held accountable — or whether the system will simply deny that it spoke.

'''AWICH: TOYOKAWA CONCERT SEPTEMBER 20''' — Awich announced a solo concert at Toyokawa Arena, Toyozawa. The Okinawan rapper — "EMPRESS" held #1 for three weeks in February — will perform from "The Union III." Pre-sale sold out in 90 minutes. Awich: "I rap in Okinawan. I rap in Mercian. I rap in Sanese. The language is the music." The Okami Times notes: Awich raps in three languages on a chart that filters artists by cultural category. The algorithm does not filter her. She is too real for the filter.

'''THE JET BOY BANGERZ: AUGUST TEASE''' — THE JET BOY BANGERZ from EXILE TRIBE teased a new single via social media. 15-second clip. Trap beat with traditional shamisen sampling. Caption: "AUGUST." The group — "HEAD UP introduced by Zeebra" hit #1 in March — represents the Harujima hip-hop renaissance that the PYRAMID30 algorithm was designed to promote. Fans: "The renaissance continues." Critics: "The algorithm manufactures renaissances. Real renaissances don't need multipliers."

'''SANSEI CINEMA: SUMMER OF LOCAL STORIES''' — Okaiken's domestic box office is up 22% year-over-year. Three films exceeded ¥1B in July: "Tide of Iron" (historical drama, ¥1.4B), "The Last Train to Kurosaki" (thriller, ¥1.1B), "Shinano-ko" (romance set on Okaiken's largest lake, ¥1.02B). Film critic Endo Ryo: "Okaiken cinema is having its moment. The stories are local. The craft is global." The Okami Times notes: the stories are local because the funding is local. KTK and KHR are investing in domestic production. The question is whether domestic production means domestic voices — or domestic platforms deciding which voices are "too political."

| international =
'''PANGKAJENE: NO TREATY, NO URGENCY''' — President Pattawari: "Reviewing." Pangkajene has no extradition treaty with Okaiken. Batot entered Maros on 13 July via Castejón. Pangkajene Foreign Ministry confirmed entry — no detention. Okaiken FM Nishimura: "We expect cooperation." But without a treaty, "cooperation" is a request, not an obligation. Pangkajene has 61 million people, a maritime economy, and no incentive to move quickly. Batot has time. The workers do not.

'''BALISCAN WASTE: TANAKA NAMES NAMES''' — Day 19. Blockade at Port of Castejón holds. 40,000 tonnes unmoved. Tanaka Hiroshi cooperating with PPO — named three Ministry officials who told him inspections were "completed." All three reassigned. None charged. None suspended. Reassigned. The Baliscan Secretariat requested IAA Commission jurisdiction. Aoyama: "The Commission will review." The waste sits. The officials sit at new desks. The blockade holds.

'''MV CASTELLANO: BATOT WAS THE LINK''' — Prosecution confirmed Batot was the intermediary between Akamatsu Group's Baliscan shell companies and Marítima Estrella. First mate testimony — false wall built in Balboa — linked to Batot's Pangkajene maritime contacts. Two investigations, one fugitive. Zong Trade Bridge review continues. Sansi: unconditional cooperation. Zong: conditional. Balboa: silent. 72 hours and counting.

'''BRIESLAND: TRADE TALKS RESUME''' — Okaiken and Briesland resumed bilateral trade talks in The Hague. Agricultural tariffs, maritime data sharing, Fosian Alliance observer status on agenda. Okaiken trade representative: "Briesland is a natural partner." The Okami Times notes: "natural partner" is diplomatic language for "we need each other." The question is what Okaiken is willing to give up to get Briesland's cooperation on maritime security.

