| editorial =
'''The system that let Batot run'''

Ronaldo Batot fled Okaiken on 12 July. Three days after the Akamatsu Group arrests. The Justice Ministry did not know he was gone until 15 July. The worldwide arrest warrant came on 16 July. Four days.

Four days in which Batot flew from Okami to Castejón, Balisca, then to Maros, Pangkajene — a country with no extradition treaty. Four days in which a central figure in two major criminal investigations crossed two international borders undetected.

The Okami Times has supported the IAA Commission, the prosecution, and the coalition's accountability agenda. We continue to support them. But the system that allowed Batot to flee is the same system that allowed the Akamatsu Group to embezzle ¥18B from 12,000 workers' pension funds for three years. The same system that allowed uninspected waste to leave Okaiken for Balisca. The same system that produced an 11-day silence from the Foreign Ministry.

Who decided Batot was a "consultant" and not a suspect? Who decided three executives were enough for 9 July? Who decided border controls did not need tightening after the arrests? These are not rhetorical questions. They have answers. The answers are in the prosecution's case file, in the Justice Ministry's decision chain, in the gap between what the system knew and what the system did.

The workers' pension funds were stolen. The intermediary fled. The system is investigating itself. The IAA Commission is resourced. The prosecution is moving. The hearing is tomorrow. These are good things. But Batot is in Maros. The waste is in Castejón. The workers are in line at a temporary office in Isshinji.

Accountability is not a press conference. It is not a warrant issued four days late. It is not a hearing scheduled for next week. Accountability is what the system does before the money disappears, before the fugitive flies, before the waste leaves the port. Everything after is repair.

The Okami Times will cover the repair. We will also cover the gap.

| okami =
'''Assembly extends Ayorin housing subsidies through September'''

The Okami Metropolitan Assembly passed an emergency resolution extending housing subsidies for 14 Ayorin families displaced by the June attacks. The subsidies were set to expire in August. Assembly members acknowledged that three months of temporary housing is insufficient but said the extension was the most they could secure under current budget constraints. Community leaders have called for a longer-term housing plan.

'''Thirteen Castellano crew members remain in custody'''

Thirteen crew members of the MV Castellano remain in custody at the Okami Port detention facility. Crew attorney Kishi Daisuke filed a motion for release, arguing that his clients were told of the cargo's nature only after departure and should be treated as couriers rather than organisers. The prosecution has indicated that cooperation with the investigation will be a factor in any release decision.

'''Habu ward conducts mountain rescue drill'''

Eighty volunteers participated in a mountain rescue drill in Habu ward, simulating a hiker evacuation at 1,200 metres in the Okami Range. The Habu fire chief said the range's proximity to the city makes preparedness essential. The drill included a helicopter extraction and a communications test with TMPD dispatch.

'''Protesters gather outside KTK headquarters over Lost Corner footage'''

Approximately 200 protesters gathered outside KTK headquarters in Chiyoda-ku, holding #TellTheTruth banners and demanding transparency over the deletion of 12 minutes of footage from the drama series Lost Corner. KTK security staff said the network respects the right to protest but has no comment on editorial decisions. Organisers said they plan to return daily until the Communications Committee releases its findings.

'''Metropolitan gymnasium prepares for Emperor's Trophy finals'''

The Okami Metropolitan Gymnasium is preparing to host the Emperor's Trophy finals from August 8 to 10. The venue has added standing room to its 12,000-seat capacity, and 1,400 volunteers have signed up to assist. The gymnasium manager said it will be the largest event hosted at the facility since the 2024 National Sports Festival.

