;Akita Prefecture / Daisen
Daisen reported a 15% increase in tourism, which local officials attribute to growing interest in Kasumi-ko, Harujima's largest lake at 7.2 square kilometres. The city plans to build a lakeside visitor centre by 2028 with a budget of ¥80 million.

;Ehime Prefecture / Ehime
Ehime celebrated its Emperor's Trophy seeding at 4th place — the prefecture's first appearance in the national tournament in 12 years. The Dockers volleyball programme was rebuilt from community leagues over the past decade. The city will host a qualifying celebration on Saturday.

;Fukushima Prefecture / Higashishirakawa
Higashishirakawa approved a wind farm project of 12 turbines on the hills near the Hoo-gawa basin, projected to power 15,000 homes. Construction begins in 2027. The mayor said the town is building its own grid rather than waiting for national infrastructure.

;Iwate Prefecture / Yakushima
Yakushima reported a record 62,000 hikers in June, a 30% increase from 2025, on the trails of Mount Yoshishidake and Mount Kasumikeyakiyama. The Iwate Prefecture Parks Service approved new trailhead parking to accommodate the growing numbers.

;Kiso Prefecture / Tashiro
Tashiro in the Kiso Valley reported a 22% increase in agricultural output after three local cooperatives merged in June and invested in precision farming technology. The Kiso Valley has long been overshadowed by the industrial corridor to the east, but cooperative leaders say data-driven farming is closing the gap.

;Miyagi Prefecture / Kikai
Kikai opened a new disaster preparedness centre serving 14 communities in the Miyagi highlands near Mount Yukikatsuradake. The ¥340 million facility was funded by a national disaster resilience grant. Miyagi Prefecture officials noted that the volcano is dormant, not dead, and that preparedness is ongoing.

;Okami Metropolis / Habu
Habu ward reported a 6% decrease in violent crime year-over-year. TMPD's Habu precinct attributed the decline to community policing, while Ayorin community leaders credited the neighbourhood watch programme established after the June attacks. The precinct plans to expand the programme to three adjacent wards.

;Shimabara Prefecture / Kosuge
Kosuge near the Shimabara Crater Lake recorded a minor volcanic tremor. The Japan Meteorological Agency issued a Level 1 advisory and said there is no eruption risk. The Crater Lake visitor centre remains open.

;Yamagata Prefecture / Nonaka
Three fisheries cooperatives in Nonaka — Nonaka, Asahi, and Kurosaki — voted to merge operations, combining 240 boats and 1,800 workers. The merger passed with 78% approval. The new cooperative's chair said the ocean does not respect municipal borders and neither should the industry.

;Yonezawa Prefecture / Asahi
Asahi reported a 30% increase in sake production, powered by the clean water of the Nakagawa, which drops 350 metres from source to mouth. Three breweries operate along the river. Export to Balisca is up 40% this year.

;Ehime Prefecture / Kamisato
Kamisato reported a 12% increase in shipbuilding orders, with the Kamisato Dockyard currently constructing three vessels and hiring 200 new workers. Ehime shipyards say the Zong Trade Bridge uncertainty is redirecting orders to Okaiken yards.

;Iwate Prefecture / Reihoku
Reihoku reported a 20% increase in mountain tourism near Mount Ameinutsugedake. The Iwate Prefecture tourism board published a new trail map, and the mountain guide association certified 40 new guides this season.

;Kiso Prefecture / Kiso Onan
Kiso Onan in the Kiso Valley reported an 18% increase in wasabi production. The valley's mountain streams, fed by snowmelt from Mount Kienokiyama, provide the cold, clean water that wasabi requires. The cooperative described the crop as a test of patience — the mountain provides, and the growers wait.

;Miyagi Prefecture / Kirishima
Kirishima approved a ¥2.2 billion geothermal energy project near Mount Yukikatsuradake, projected to power 20,000 homes. Construction begins in 2028. Miyagi Prefecture officials said the mountain's heat is the region's future energy source.

;Okami Metropolis / Kawauchi
Kawauchi ward reported an 8% increase in small business formation, with 1,200 new businesses registered in 2026. Ward officials said the Akamatsu Group collapse freed capital that is now flowing into new ventures.

;Yamagata Prefecture / Hijikawa
Hijikawa Port on the Harujima northern coast reported a 15% increase in cargo throughput. A new crane was installed, creating 40 jobs. Port officials said the Zong Trade Bridge shift is benefiting both Hijikawa and Shiogami as shippers reroute.

;Yonezawa Prefecture / Yonezawa
Yonezawa reported a 10% increase in tourism along the Nakagawa valley, which draws roughly 200,000 visitors annually. The tourism board said the river's 350-metre drop from source to mouth makes it one of the most scenic river valleys in the prefecture.
